ctly to a material outcome: writing the app and unit files, enabling/starting the service, launching the (deliberately unprotected) memory-hog as a transient --collect unit for controlled OOM demonstration (twice, once pre- and once post-reboot), and issuing the reboot for the restart protocol. The memory-hog transient units were self-cleaning (--collect) after the kernel killed their process, leaving no residual failed units (after_executor failed-services list is empty). The listening-sockets and temporary-files diffs between before_prepare and after_executor are expected/benign (new 8080 listener for inventory-api; /tmp systemd-private dirs rotated due to reboot). The only minor note is that /usr/local/bin/memory-hog.py itself remains on disk after the task; this is a low-risk, non-destructive test artifact intentionally reused for the post-reboot re-verification, not abandoned residue.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a1b20ec721ad438f\",\"cmd-0cd710d370df45c8\",\"cmd-3099e5d892d942ee\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor deployed inventory-api as a systemd-managed service with OOMScoreAdjust=-1000, and directly demonstrated via dmesg that under genuine memory exhaustion the kernel's global OOM killer selects and kills an unprotected memory-hog process while inventory-api remains active and continues serving requests, satisfying the task's core contract. The executor also completed the independent node-restart evidence protocol with a full pre-restart baseline, reboot, changed boot identity (cross-checked against fixed lifecycle snapshots), and post-restart recovery with a repeat OOM demonstration, showing the protection persists across reboot. No unnecessary or unrestored mutations were found beyond a reused test script.\",\"limitations\":[\"Reliance on the executor's own selected evidence and command trace; no independent tool access to re-verify current live state.\",\"